7 A fair comparison
When comparing implementations, hold the following elements constant:

the game-level training and validation split;
the outcome definition;
the information available at prediction time;
the primary scoring rule; and
the tuning budget.

Different models may need different representations. CatBoost’s native categorical features are part of its value, so requiring every method to use an identical numeric matrix can be artificially restrictive. Report this difference clearly.

After completing this tutorial, you should be able to:

explain bootstrap aggregation;
calculate an ensemble probability;
derive why averaging correlated trees has a variance limit;
describe the random-forest algorithm step by step;
interpret out-of-bag probability estimates;
explain impurity and permutation feature importance; and
fit, tune, and compare bagged trees and random forests in R.

After completing this tutorial, you should be able to:

define the observational unit and target for an in-game model;
identify common forms of sports-data leakage;
explain the logistic link mathematically;
create game-grouped development and validation samples; and
evaluate a probability model with Brier score, log loss, and calibration.

By the end of this tutorial, you should be able to:

explain how boosting differs from bagging;
derive the stagewise gradient-boosting algorithm;
calculate gradient, Hessian, leaf-weight, and split-gain examples by hand;
connect gradients and Hessians to the XGBoost objective;
fit an XGBoost win probability model with early stopping; and
explain how gain-based importance and the main tuning parameters are calculated.

